esults in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186508977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12433c4a-668f-57da-9582-b6ee6515dc42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id 17592186508415} {:answer/id #uuid "5c05a8fd-c462-59f0-82be-578cd7e2ef16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id 17592186508526} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a54554-40d8-5b1a-a681-1f39ccace6c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id 17592186508691} {:answer/id #uuid "d353fe5f-d6bc-5d38-8a44-d344cb894fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id 17592186508730} {:answer/id #uuid "f972d2aa-60ae-5ae2-a173-f83d6fe3630e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id 17592186508802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c05a8fd-c462-59f0-82be-578cd7e2ef16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id 17592186508526} :answer-field/id #uuid "53289756-546a-50f2-80e0-c0a1e0a48184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186508836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9126714-6e57-5593-b5b4-4801efbfb113" :question/math-academy-id "q-95111" :question/problem "Given that $q(x,y)=xy,$ find $\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $q(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂q}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂q}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂q}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂q}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $q$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂q}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂q}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}
